f 17592186045417] [17592186503580 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503580 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503580 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503580 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503580 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503580 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503580 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503580 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186503232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503580 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "83693d58-e295-516a-afbd-27422e50c864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503585 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503585 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503585 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503585 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503585 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503585 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503585 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503585 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186503095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503585 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8ea658b1-8a5c-53c8-b110-9aef693ebefd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503606 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503606 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503606 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503606 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503606 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186503461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503606 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503606 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503606 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186503295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503606 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b7a9a4a0-2fea-53c4-88e7-914e96e2af54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503657 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503657 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503657 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503657 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\text{Var}[X]=1,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[7-\\sqrt{5}X].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503657 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14c37ea2-4835-508a-983a-3cccd74633e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503657 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[7-\\sqrt{5}X] & ={(-\\sqrt{5})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503695 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503695 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503695 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute $P(-1<X<1)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{28}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -2\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503695 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6487d2c0-d995-5efa-98e2-2110fb813c4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503695 #edb/ref 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X<1) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{28}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}([\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+(1)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503728 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503728 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503728 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac3ed9ab-9e20-50c1-a2df-d0e7acffd405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503728 #edb/ref 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+8{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +4)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =-4.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-2x}$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{-6x}$ is not a solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503757 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186503543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503757 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503757 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503757 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/50/50a06e9bb2865ea9ce3314700d89de3cd12139dee72ddcf2652fb868e88abbcb.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503757 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb704702-8992-5802-a3da-c6412351672c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503757 #edb/ref 1074 "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve II is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve III is not simple.\n\n- Curve IV is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503758 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01f99ab6-ea0a-570b-aeb6-695dc00afd24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503758 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186395500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d6acb7c-f7cd-511c-b977-1c4f19622515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503767 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13e0f70b-698d-55d0-bc47-57987cc04fec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186503728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503768 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503790 #edb/ref 17592186045417]